message: "If you use this software, Lean 4 finite checks, or verification archive, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Computational Apolarity Experiments, W(D4) Spinor Combinatorics, and Decidable Lean 4.35.0 Finite Checks for Hodge Conjecture Testbeds"
type: software
version: "0.7.0"
date-released: "2026-11-15"
license: CC-BY-4.0
authors:
  - family-names: "Munawer"
    given-names: "Qamar"
    affiliation: "Brandenburg University of Technology Cottbus-Senftenberg"
abstract: >-
  Reproducible exact rational and modular (F_p) computational experiment toolkit (zero-float Python
  modules, scan_forbidden == []) and core Lean 4.35.0 finite combinatorial/degree-counting checks
  (43 decidable `by rfl` declarations with zero Mathlib imports and axioms: []; NOT a formalization
  of schemes, cohomology, or Hodge theory) for HODGE-MILLENNIUM-001 testbeds on sextic Calabi-Yau
  fourfolds and Mumford spinor weight sets. Classical theorems (Griffiths, Shioda-Aoki-Ran, Macaulay,
  Peskine-Szpiro, Deligne/Andre, Wang) are attributed to their original authors. Standing epistemic
  invariant: GLOBAL_HODGE_CONJECTURE = OPEN, S-BRIDGE-DEFORM-CY4 = ADVERSARIALLY_BLOCKED_OPEN_BRIDGE,
  S-BRIDGE-SPINOR-MOTIVE = ADVERSARIALLY_BLOCKED_OPEN_BRIDGE, release_authorized = false,
  theorem_progress = UNKNOWN.
keywords:
  - hodge-conjecture
  - lean4
  - computational-algebraic-geometry
  - calabi-yau-fourfolds
  - abelian-varieties
  - spin8-triality
  - macaulay-apolarity
  - shioda-lefschetz
  - math-gov
